Track 2: Azimuth Error Over Lake Inari
- Story & Context: Hunched over a kerosene-lit mixing console on the frozen shoreline of Lake Inari, tape manipulator Matti Harju admitted to his collaborators that he had deliberately scrubbed his ex-fiancée’s recorded voice by dragging a neodymium magnet across their shared cassette collection. He attempted to smother the lingering tape hiss with glassy DX7 electric piano chords and an agonizingly slow lap-steel melody, but his cassette player kept slipping on a degraded rubber belt, creating spaced, lumbering rhythm drops that felt like an ancient brown bear fighting off an unseasonable winter awakening. The bitter metallic smell of oxidized iron flakes drifted across his frostbitten knuckles as he wept over the faders, unable to erase the ghost frequencies her microphone had printed onto the plastic shell. In a cracked, pitch-shifted baritone captured through a dying carbon mic, he whispered his confession over the glacial slide chords: "You went under the ice, and left me with the noise floor."
